A bodyless component renders from its override, and the declaration itself
contributes nothing.

`component gap(w int) ui.node` with no block is a signature: there is no body
to specialize away, so this golden is what says the override's body reaches
the output rather than the emptiness the base declaration has. Before the
block was optional, saying this needed `{}` on the base -- an empty body,
which is a declaration that renders nothing and is not the same thing.

The `.sngl` fixture of the same name carries the checker-level assertion and a
`SKIP(codegen)`, because a per-platform harness registers only its own
platform and cannot resolve `sngl:platform/html`.

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:platform/html"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component gap(w int) ui.node

component gap[html.platform](w) {
    ui.text(value="gap {w}")
}

ui.window {
    ui.vbox {
        gap(w=4)
    }
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
<!-- Code generated by sngl (html) from app.sngl; DO NOT EDIT. -->

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <style>
* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head><body>

<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column">
  <span>gap 4</span>
</div>

<script>
(() => {
  // sngl-entry.js
  var state = {};
})();
</script>
</body></html>
